<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext carve-out for the auto-failover health probe, and NOTHING else.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    ====================
    `failover/Http204HealthProbe` dials `ConfigBuilder.HEALTH_PROBE_TARGET_URL` with
    HttpURLConnection, which Android's NetworkSecurityPolicy governs. The app is targetSdk 36 and at
    targetSdk >= 28 the platform default is cleartext DENIED, so a plaintext target failed EVERY
    probe with `IOException: Cleartext HTTP traffic ... not permitted`. The probe reports a throw as
    "unhealthy", so the watchdog read a perfectly healthy tunnel as dead and answered with a rotation
    storm and a give-up over working servers. Device-verified on SM-S918B / Android 15.

    WHY CLEARTEXT RATHER THAN JUST USING https://
    =============================================
    Switching the probe to https removes the defect but costs camouflage, and this is a
    censorship-circumvention tool where that matters more than it would elsewhere:

      * When there is NO tunnel (the UNPROTECTED give-up, or the window between tunnels) the probe
        travels the CLEAR network, where DPI can see it. A plaintext `GET /generate_204` to
        cp.cloudflare.com is indistinguishable from Android's own captive-portal check, which is
        cleartext BY DESIGN so that portals can intercept it. A TLS handshake to the same host is the
        anomaly — it marks the device as running something that probes on a schedule.
      * Inside the tunnel, a periodic ~5 KB TLS handshake is a far more distinctive traffic-analysis
        signature than a ~300 byte plaintext request/response pair.

    WHY NOT android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
    ===========================================
    That would re-permit plaintext for the WHOLE app. This file permits it for exactly one hostname
    and leaves every other destination denied by the platform default. There is deliberately no
    <base-config> element: omitting it leaves the defaults (cleartext denied, system trust anchors,
    user CAs untrusted) exactly as the platform sets them, so this file cannot loosen TLS trust as a
    side effect of loosening cleartext for one host.

    KEEPING IT IN SYNC
    ==================
    The domain below MUST equal `ConfigBuilder.HEALTH_PROBE_HOST`. If you change that constant and
    not this file, the probe silently returns to failing every call — the exact defect above.
    `HealthProbeSchemeTest` couples the two and fails if they drift.

    NOT related to the Ping Test target (`state/PingTester.PING_TEST_TARGET`), which is also
    cleartext but is dialled by MeasureLatency in the Go bridge — raw native sockets that
    NetworkSecurityPolicy does not govern at all. It needs no entry here and gains nothing from one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">cp.cloudflare.com</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
